WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.855
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.855 --> 00:00:06.960
define static methods—named operations in a

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.281
class—supply typed inputs, return results, and

00:00:10.281 --> 00:00:13.603
distinguish those results from printed output.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:17.473
Keeping a calculation in one reusable method

00:00:17.473 --> 00:00:20.832
lets different parts of an application use the

00:00:20.832 --> 00:00:24.337
same rule, choose how to display its result, and

00:00:24.337 --> 00:00:26.235
test changes in one place.

00:00:26.906 --> 00:00:29.709
A campus workshop needs totals for supply

00:00:29.709 --> 00:00:30.188
orders.

00:00:30.188 --> 00:00:33.265
Each order gives a whole-number item quantity

00:00:33.265 --> 00:00:35.453
and a price in dollars per item.

00:00:35.453 --> 00:00:38.461
We'll build one method that multiplies those

00:00:38.461 --> 00:00:41.196
inputs and returns the total in dollars.

00:00:41.196 --> 00:00:44.478
The caller, the code requesting the calculation,

00:00:44.478 --> 00:00:46.256
will decide what to print.

00:00:46.919 --> 00:00:49.937
Success means different orders use the same

00:00:49.937 --> 00:00:52.885
calculation and receive their own results.

00:00:52.885 --> 00:00:55.974
We'll first work through example calls, then

00:00:55.974 --> 00:00:59.062
look at a copied numeric input and an output

00:00:59.062 --> 00:00:59.554
action.

00:00:59.554 --> 00:01:02.923
Finally, you'll predict two new totals before we

00:01:02.923 --> 00:01:05.169
run the completed order program.

00:01:05.224 --> 00:01:08.538
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace and give

00:01:08.538 --> 00:01:10.699
the shared calculation a name.

00:01:14.726 --> 00:01:17.644
PriceTools is a class, a container for this

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:18.119
helper.

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:21.037
Its static method can be called through the

00:01:21.037 --> 00:01:23.683
class name, without creating a separate

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:24.905
PriceTools object.

00:01:24.905 --> 00:01:28.094
We'll name the operation orderTotal and declare

00:01:28.094 --> 00:01:29.451
the inputs it needs.

00:01:36.736 --> 00:01:39.455
Quantity and unitPrice are parameters, the

00:01:39.455 --> 00:01:41.461
method's local input variables.

00:01:41.461 --> 00:01:44.503
Their types describe a whole-number count and a

00:01:44.503 --> 00:01:45.410
decimal price.

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:48.193
The return type tells a caller what kind of

00:01:48.193 --> 00:01:49.293
result to expect.

00:01:49.293 --> 00:01:52.271
Here, double before the method name declares a

00:01:52.271 --> 00:01:53.759
decimal numeric result.

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:56.802
Let's put the common multiplication in the body

00:01:56.802 --> 00:01:58.161
and return its value.

00:02:02.548 --> 00:02:05.535
Return evaluates the product, ends this method

00:02:05.535 --> 00:02:08.198
call, and sends the number to its caller.

00:02:08.198 --> 00:02:09.302
It doesn't print.

00:02:09.302 --> 00:02:12.354
Declaring this helper hasn't performed an order

00:02:12.354 --> 00:02:15.471
calculation yet; we still need calls that supply

00:02:15.471 --> 00:02:15.925
values.

00:02:15.925 --> 00:02:18.003
We'll place those calls in Main.

00:02:18.003 --> 00:02:20.926
Main is the program's starting class, and its

00:02:20.926 --> 00:02:23.848
main method is where this application begins.

00:02:29.157 --> 00:02:32.229
Our first example order has two items at three

00:02:32.229 --> 00:02:33.097
dollars each.

00:02:33.097 --> 00:02:36.035
Arguments are the values supplied at a call.

00:02:36.035 --> 00:02:39.106
Their positions match the declared parameters:

00:02:39.106 --> 00:02:42.245
two goes to quantity, and three point zero goes

00:02:42.245 --> 00:02:43.113
to unitPrice.

00:02:43.113 --> 00:02:44.916
The product is six dollars.

00:02:44.916 --> 00:02:47.854
We'll store that returned number in a caller

00:02:47.854 --> 00:02:49.857
variable before displaying it.

00:02:57.239 --> 00:02:59.176
Total belongs to the caller.

00:02:59.176 --> 00:03:02.219
The assignment receives the completed method

00:03:02.219 --> 00:03:04.779
result, and println then reads total.

00:03:04.779 --> 00:03:07.614
A second order will use four items at two

00:03:07.614 --> 00:03:08.514
dollars each.

00:03:08.514 --> 00:03:10.658
It should return eight dollars.

00:03:10.658 --> 00:03:13.978
This time we'll pass the returned value directly

00:03:13.978 --> 00:03:14.739
to println.

00:03:22.532 --> 00:03:25.334
Each call gets fresh parameter values.

00:03:25.334 --> 00:03:28.799
Java completes the inner calculation before the

00:03:28.799 --> 00:03:30.789
outer println can print it.

00:03:30.789 --> 00:03:34.107
Let's click Run to check both caller choices.

00:03:39.236 --> 00:03:41.919
The first line is six point zero, and the second

00:03:41.919 --> 00:03:43.037
is eight point zero.

00:03:43.037 --> 00:03:45.719
One result was stored before printing; the other

00:03:45.719 --> 00:03:47.116
went directly to println.

00:03:47.116 --> 00:03:49.576
Both came from the same multiplication rule.

00:03:49.576 --> 00:03:51.923
Inspect the helper body: it reads only its

00:03:51.923 --> 00:03:53.767
parameters and returns a product.

00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:56.450
It doesn't print or change state outside itself.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:59.021
That makes this calculation pure; two matching

00:03:59.021 --> 00:04:01.536
observations alone wouldn't establish purity.

00:04:01.611 --> 00:04:04.857
A method's input variable is separate from the

00:04:04.857 --> 00:04:06.128
caller's variable.

00:04:06.128 --> 00:04:09.374
Let's replace this example with a small helper

00:04:09.374 --> 00:04:11.844
that adds one to a copied quantity.

00:04:11.844 --> 00:04:14.879
This makes the separation visible before we

00:04:14.879 --> 00:04:16.361
return to the orders.

00:04:25.947 --> 00:04:29.075
Java uses pass by value: a parameter receives a

00:04:29.075 --> 00:04:30.873
copy of the supplied value.

00:04:30.873 --> 00:04:33.402
For an integer, that copy is a number.

00:04:33.402 --> 00:04:36.331
The assignment inside addOne changes its own

00:04:36.331 --> 00:04:37.595
quantity parameter.

00:04:37.595 --> 00:04:40.191
It doesn't assign to a caller variable.

00:04:40.191 --> 00:04:43.253
We'll start the caller's requested quantity at

00:04:43.253 --> 00:04:46.315
three and store the returned result separately

00:04:46.315 --> 00:04:47.047
in revised.

00:04:59.362 --> 00:05:02.763
The parameter receives three, changes its local

00:05:02.763 --> 00:05:05.006
copy to four, and returns four.

00:05:05.006 --> 00:05:07.538
Revised stores that returned value.

00:05:07.538 --> 00:05:10.577
Requested has never been reassigned, so it

00:05:10.577 --> 00:05:12.169
should still be three.

00:05:12.169 --> 00:05:15.353
We'll print the two caller variables in that

00:05:15.353 --> 00:05:15.787
order.

00:05:22.717 --> 00:05:25.998
Let's run this complete program and inspect the

00:05:25.998 --> 00:05:28.092
two values left in the caller.

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:35.520
Requested prints three, while revised prints

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:35.855
four.

00:05:35.855 --> 00:05:38.676
Returning didn't automatically replace the

00:05:38.676 --> 00:05:40.489
original argument variable.

00:05:40.489 --> 00:05:43.511
To change requested, the caller would have to

00:05:43.511 --> 00:05:45.660
store the returned result there.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.816
This example traces numeric values; it does not

00:05:48.816 --> 00:05:51.972
mean that Java copies an entire object whenever

00:05:51.972 --> 00:05:53.315
an object is passed.

00:05:53.449 --> 00:05:56.647
A calculation can supply a result, but another

00:05:56.647 --> 00:05:59.080
method may be useful for an action.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:02.347
We'll replace the numeric helper with a display

00:06:02.347 --> 00:06:02.833
method.

00:06:02.833 --> 00:06:06.031
The keyword void means the call sends no value

00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:06.379
back.

00:06:06.379 --> 00:06:09.368
The print in this body is a side effect, an

00:06:09.368 --> 00:06:12.357
observable action beyond providing a return

00:06:12.357 --> 00:06:12.774
value.

00:06:22.657 --> 00:06:25.878
ShowTotal receives a number and displays it with

00:06:25.878 --> 00:06:26.414
a label.

00:06:26.414 --> 00:06:29.434
We will put a caller message before and after

00:06:29.434 --> 00:06:32.520
the call so we can see when control enters the

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.332
method and when it returns.

00:06:34.332 --> 00:06:37.485
The void call stands alone, because there is no

00:06:37.485 --> 00:06:40.370
returned value to assign or give to another

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:40.907
println.

00:07:01.176 --> 00:07:04.146
The caller should print its first message, the

00:07:04.146 --> 00:07:07.181
method should print the labeled total, and then

00:07:07.181 --> 00:07:09.764
the caller should continue with its last

00:07:09.764 --> 00:07:10.281
message.

00:07:10.281 --> 00:07:12.476
Let's run it to follow that order.

00:07:16.932 --> 00:07:20.175
We see the caller's Before display, the method's

00:07:20.175 --> 00:07:23.080
Total line, and the caller's After display.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:26.323
The body finished and control returned without a

00:07:26.323 --> 00:07:27.201
result value.

00:07:27.201 --> 00:07:30.173
Void doesn't require a method to print; this

00:07:30.173 --> 00:07:31.592
particular body does.

00:07:31.592 --> 00:07:34.430
Likewise, static doesn't guarantee purity.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:37.132
Inspect what an operation actually does.

00:07:37.269 --> 00:07:40.644
Now we'll return to the workshop calculation and

00:07:40.644 --> 00:07:42.473
keep output in the caller.

00:07:42.473 --> 00:07:45.708
We'll restore the same PriceTools method, then

00:07:45.708 --> 00:07:48.661
supply two new orders for your prediction.

00:07:48.661 --> 00:07:50.419
The rule has not changed.

00:08:02.529 --> 00:08:05.839
The first order has three items at four dollars

00:08:05.839 --> 00:08:07.318
and fifty cents each.

00:08:07.318 --> 00:08:10.418
The second has five items at two dollars and

00:08:10.418 --> 00:08:11.615
fifty cents each.

00:08:11.615 --> 00:08:14.573
We'll call the helper once for each order,

00:08:14.573 --> 00:08:17.461
leaving both print statements in the main

00:08:17.461 --> 00:08:17.954
method.

00:08:33.163 --> 00:08:36.368
Before we run this final program, write the two

00:08:36.368 --> 00:08:38.687
output lines you expect, in order.

00:08:38.687 --> 00:08:41.893
Match each argument to its parameter, calculate

00:08:41.893 --> 00:08:45.030
the product, and decide where printing occurs.

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:47.962
Use the earlier worked calls as your model.

00:08:47.962 --> 00:08:50.213
Pause here if you want more time.

00:08:58.308 --> 00:09:01.560
Let's click Run and compare both returned totals

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:02.983
with your prediction.

00:09:08.077 --> 00:09:10.791
The program prints thirteen point five, then

00:09:10.791 --> 00:09:11.901
twelve point five.

00:09:11.901 --> 00:09:14.861
Three items at four dollars and fifty cents cost

00:09:14.861 --> 00:09:16.896
thirteen dollars and fifty cents.

00:09:16.896 --> 00:09:19.733
Five items at two dollars and fifty cents cost

00:09:19.733 --> 00:09:21.645
twelve dollars and fifty cents.

00:09:21.645 --> 00:09:24.173
Each call received its own parameters and

00:09:24.173 --> 00:09:25.715
returned its own product.

00:09:25.715 --> 00:09:28.182
The caller printed each result after the

00:09:28.182 --> 00:09:29.477
calculation finished.

00:09:29.644 --> 00:09:32.444
We've built one reusable rule and checked it

00:09:32.444 --> 00:09:33.780
with changing inputs.

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:36.516
Typed parameters state what the calculation

00:09:36.516 --> 00:09:38.934
needs, and return supplies the result.

00:09:38.934 --> 00:09:41.670
The caller can store or display that value.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:44.724
The numeric-copy example showed that a parameter

00:09:44.724 --> 00:09:47.460
assignment is local, while the void example

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.324
showed an action that returns control without

00:09:50.324 --> 00:09:51.469
returning a value.

00:09:52.228 --> 00:09:55.525
Now use the notebook to build a discounted total

00:09:55.525 --> 00:09:56.006
method.

00:09:56.006 --> 00:09:59.028
Keep the calculation separate from printing,

00:09:59.028 --> 00:10:02.187
subtract the flat discount once, and test both

00:10:02.187 --> 00:10:05.003
stated orders and the zero-quantity case.

00:10:05.003 --> 00:10:08.094
Explain why the chosen inputs are allowed and

00:10:08.094 --> 00:10:11.185
why the body supports calling the calculation

00:10:11.185 --> 00:10:11.528
pure.
